1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,979
A winter day in a Japanese prison camp.

2
00:00:02,999 --> 00:00:06,071
A guard opens a cell and marches out an

3
00:00:06,091 --> 00:00:07,446
American prisoner, stripped naked.

4
00:00:07,466 --> 00:00:10,194
He walks him onto the parade ground and

5
00:00:10,214 --> 00:00:12,256
leaves him standing in the snow.

6
00:00:12,276 --> 00:00:15,348
The prisoner is told he can go back inside

7
00:00:15,368 --> 00:00:17,430
when he tells them the code.

8
00:00:18,725 --> 00:00:21,029
He later said it was twenty-seven degrees,

9
00:00:21,049 --> 00:00:23,684
and the snow was about six inches deep.

10
00:00:23,704 --> 00:00:26,340
The guard told him that if he moved,

11
00:00:26,360 --> 00:00:27,667
he would shoot him.

12
00:00:27,687 --> 00:00:29,659
He stood there for an hour.

13
00:00:29,679 --> 00:00:31,982
When they finally pushed him back toward

14
00:00:32,002 --> 00:00:35,302
his cell, his bare feet had frozen to the ground.

15
00:00:35,322 --> 00:00:38,621
In his own words, I left my soles on that

16
00:00:38,641 --> 00:00:39,441
parade ground.

17
00:00:40,625 --> 00:00:42,845
The prisoner was Joe Kieyoomia,

18
00:00:42,865 --> 00:00:45,022
a Navajo silversmith from the American

19
00:00:45,042 --> 00:00:47,199
Southwest, drafted into the United States

20
00:00:47,219 --> 00:00:49,685
Army in 1941.

21
00:00:49,705 --> 00:00:52,471
The code the Japanese wanted was being

22
00:00:52,491 --> 00:00:54,859
spoken on American radios across the

23
00:00:54,879 --> 00:00:56,849
Pacific, in his own language.

24
00:00:56,869 --> 00:00:59,279
And Joe Kieyoomia could not understand a

25
00:00:59,299 --> 00:01:00,165
word of it.

26
00:01:01,375 --> 00:01:04,129
This is the true story of the Navajo code:

27
00:01:04,149 --> 00:01:06,286
the twenty-nine young men who built it,

28
00:01:06,306 --> 00:01:08,135
the Marines who carried it from

29
00:01:08,155 --> 00:01:10,292
Guadalcanal to Iwo Jima, and the one

30
00:01:10,312 --> 00:01:12,141
Navajo prisoner the Japanese tried to

31
00:01:12,161 --> 00:01:13,066
break it with.

32
00:01:13,086 --> 00:01:15,698
Most of what we know about Kieyoomia comes

33
00:01:15,718 --> 00:01:18,242
from his own interviews, given fifty years

34
00:01:18,262 --> 00:01:21,148
later, and on some points his accounts and

35
00:01:21,168 --> 00:01:22,238
the records disagree.

36
00:01:22,258 --> 00:01:24,075
We will tell you where.

37
00:01:25,750 --> 00:01:28,214
To understand this story, you have to

38
00:01:28,234 --> 00:01:29,633
start in the classrooms.

39
00:01:29,653 --> 00:01:31,762
In the years before the war,

40
00:01:31,782 --> 00:01:33,891
many Navajo children were sent to

41
00:01:33,911 --> 00:01:34,955
government boarding schools.

42
00:01:34,975 --> 00:01:37,794
Chester Nez, who would become one of the

43
00:01:37,814 --> 00:01:40,632
first code talkers, was sent away at eight.

44
00:01:40,652 --> 00:01:42,761
He remembered that the children were

45
00:01:42,781 --> 00:01:44,910
forbidden to speak Navajo at all.

46
00:01:46,175 --> 00:01:48,425
The daughter of another code talker,

47
00:01:48,445 --> 00:01:51,451
the poet Laura Tohe, has described how her

48
00:01:51,471 --> 00:01:53,343
father's school punished students for

49
00:01:53,363 --> 00:01:54,856
using their own language.

50
00:01:54,876 --> 00:01:57,882
She calls what happened next a huge contradiction.

51
00:01:57,902 --> 00:01:59,774
Another code talker, Thomas Begay,

52
00:01:59,794 --> 00:02:02,422
did not learn English until he was

53
00:02:02,442 --> 00:02:03,955
thirteen, at boarding school.

54
00:02:05,225 --> 00:02:08,704
In 1942, there were about fifty thousand

55
00:02:08,724 --> 00:02:09,431
Navajo people.

56
00:02:09,451 --> 00:02:11,977
Their language had no written alphabet of

57
00:02:11,997 --> 00:02:12,704
its own.

58
00:02:12,724 --> 00:02:15,250
Outside the tribe, very few people on

59
00:02:15,270 --> 00:02:16,705
earth could speak it.

60
00:02:16,725 --> 00:02:19,978
A Navy fact sheet later put the number of

61
00:02:19,998 --> 00:02:22,161
non-Navajos who understood it at the

62
00:02:22,181 --> 00:02:24,706
outbreak of war at fewer than thirty.

63
00:02:24,726 --> 00:02:26,545
None of them were Japanese.

64
00:02:28,050 --> 00:02:31,252
Joe Kieyoomia grew up in that world.

65
00:02:31,272 --> 00:02:34,000
He was born on the Navajo reservation,

66
00:02:34,020 --> 00:02:35,570
most likely in Arizona.

67
00:02:35,590 --> 00:02:37,140
The year is uncertain:

68
00:02:37,160 --> 00:02:40,190
his Army record says 1917,

69
00:02:40,210 --> 00:02:43,150
other sources say 1919.

70
00:02:43,170 --> 00:02:45,997
In March 1941, at Santa Fe,

71
00:02:46,017 --> 00:02:48,174
New Mexico, he was drafted.

72
00:02:48,194 --> 00:02:51,223
The Army clerk filled in his card:

73
00:02:51,243 --> 00:02:53,837
single, three years of high school,

74
00:02:53,857 --> 00:02:55,143
American Indian, citizen.

75
00:02:55,163 --> 00:02:56,014
Civilian job:

76
00:02:56,034 --> 00:02:56,834
silversmith.

77
00:02:58,125 --> 00:03:01,329
Kieyoomia was sent to fill out a New

78
00:03:01,349 --> 00:03:02,828
Mexico National Guard regiment,

79
00:03:02,848 --> 00:03:04,702
the two hundredth Coast Artillery,

80
00:03:04,722 --> 00:03:05,826
an anti-aircraft unit.

81
00:03:05,846 --> 00:03:08,450
At Fort Bliss in Texas, draftees doubled

82
00:03:08,470 --> 00:03:10,699
it to about eighteen hundred men.

83
00:03:10,719 --> 00:03:13,322
Almost all of its men spoke Spanish.

84
00:03:13,342 --> 00:03:16,321
That was one reason the Army chose them

85
00:03:16,341 --> 00:03:17,465
for the Philippines.

86
00:03:18,900 --> 00:03:21,764
They landed there in September 1941 and

87
00:03:21,784 --> 00:03:24,169
set up their guns around Clark Field,

88
00:03:24,189 --> 00:03:25,200
north of Manila.

89
00:03:25,220 --> 00:03:28,278
Kieyoomia served in the headquarters

90
00:03:28,298 --> 00:03:30,276
battery of the second battalion.

91
00:03:30,296 --> 00:03:32,273
On the eighth of December,

92
00:03:32,293 --> 00:03:34,670
a few hours after Pearl Harbor,

93
00:03:34,690 --> 00:03:37,067
Japanese bombers came over Clark Field.

94
00:03:37,087 --> 00:03:39,464
The New Mexico gunners fired back.

95
00:03:39,484 --> 00:03:43,080
State records still call them the first to fire.

96
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:46,504
In the weeks after Pearl Harbor,

97
00:03:46,524 --> 00:03:48,982
one of the regiment's battalions was split

98
00:03:49,002 --> 00:03:51,814
off in Manila to form a second unit,

99
00:03:51,834 --> 00:03:54,292
the five hundred and fifteenth Coast Artillery.

100
00:03:54,312 --> 00:03:56,770
New Mexico calls it the first American

101
00:03:56,790 --> 00:03:59,956
unit born in battle in the Second World War.

102
00:03:59,976 --> 00:04:02,434
By the state's count, the New Mexico

103
00:04:02,454 --> 00:04:04,204
gunners shot down eighty-six enemy

104
00:04:04,224 --> 00:04:05,640
aircraft before the end.

105
00:04:06,925 --> 00:04:08,874
For four months, the Americans and

106
00:04:08,894 --> 00:04:10,843
Filipinos held out on the Bataan

107
00:04:10,863 --> 00:04:12,155
peninsula, hungry and sick,

108
00:04:12,175 --> 00:04:14,124
with no help on the way.

109
00:04:14,144 --> 00:04:17,345
On the ninth of April 1942,

110
00:04:17,365 --> 00:04:19,781
General Edward King surrendered Bataan.

111
00:04:19,801 --> 00:04:22,218
The two hundredth Coast Artillery,

112
00:04:22,238 --> 00:04:24,992
Joe Kieyoomia with it, became prisoners of

113
00:04:25,012 --> 00:04:26,505
the Japanese Fourteenth Army.

114
00:04:27,800 --> 00:04:30,851
General King is said to have told his men

115
00:04:30,871 --> 00:04:33,239
to remember that they had not surrendered.

116
00:04:33,259 --> 00:04:36,310
They had no choice but to obey his order.

117
00:04:36,330 --> 00:04:38,358
It put the New Mexico regiment,

118
00:04:38,378 --> 00:04:40,551
and Joe Kieyoomia, in Japanese hands for

119
00:04:40,571 --> 00:04:41,960
the rest of the war.

120
00:04:43,225 --> 00:04:46,668
What followed is known as the Bataan Death March.

121
00:04:46,688 --> 00:04:48,208
Around seventy-five thousand prisoners,

122
00:04:48,228 --> 00:04:50,901
most of them Filipino and about twelve

123
00:04:50,921 --> 00:04:52,825
thousand American, were marched north

124
00:04:52,845 --> 00:04:54,749
toward a camp called O'Donnell.

125
00:04:54,769 --> 00:04:58,213
Estimates of the dead on the road run from

126
00:04:58,233 --> 00:05:00,137
five thousand to eighteen thousand

127
00:05:00,157 --> 00:05:02,831
Filipinos and five hundred to six hundred

128
00:05:02,851 --> 00:05:04,005
and fifty Americans.

129
00:05:05,375 --> 00:05:07,382
Kieyoomia survived the march.

130
00:05:07,402 --> 00:05:11,173
A Navy fact sheet from 1992 notes that

131
00:05:11,193 --> 00:05:13,478
about twenty Navajos were serving with the

132
00:05:13,498 --> 00:05:14,795
Army in the Philippines.

133
00:05:14,815 --> 00:05:17,852
Kieyoomia was one of them.

134
00:05:17,872 --> 00:05:20,332
Of the eighteen hundred New Mexicans who

135
00:05:20,352 --> 00:05:22,812
went to the Philippines, about half would

136
00:05:22,832 --> 00:05:23,895
never come home.

137
00:05:25,575 --> 00:05:27,211
Seven thousand miles away,

138
00:05:27,231 --> 00:05:30,109
in Los Angeles, a civil engineer named

139
00:05:30,129 --> 00:05:32,593
Philip Johnston was reading the newspaper.

140
00:05:32,613 --> 00:05:36,195
It was early 1942.

141
00:05:36,215 --> 00:05:39,352
The story said the Army had used a few

142
00:05:39,372 --> 00:05:41,458
Native American soldiers to send messages

143
00:05:41,477 --> 00:05:43,562
in their own languages during training

144
00:05:43,582 --> 00:05:44,635
exercises in Louisiana.

145
00:05:46,175 --> 00:05:48,640
Johnston was the son of a missionary.

146
00:05:48,660 --> 00:05:51,480
He had grown up on the Navajo reservation

147
00:05:51,500 --> 00:05:53,975
near Leupp, Arizona, and learned Navajo

148
00:05:53,995 --> 00:05:55,495
playing with Navajo children.

149
00:05:55,515 --> 00:05:58,915
As a boy of about nine, he translated for

150
00:05:58,935 --> 00:06:01,195
a Navajo delegation that went to

151
00:06:01,215 --> 00:06:03,475
Washington to see President Theodore Roosevelt.

152
00:06:03,495 --> 00:06:07,275
He had served in France in the First World War.

153
00:06:07,295 --> 00:06:10,715
And he knew how hard Navajo was for outsiders.

154
00:06:12,150 --> 00:06:14,312
He took his idea to the Navy,

155
00:06:14,332 --> 00:06:16,805
and was sent to the Marines at Camp

156
00:06:16,825 --> 00:06:18,052
Elliott, near San Diego.

157
00:06:18,072 --> 00:06:21,070
On the twenty-eighth of February 1942,

158
00:06:21,090 --> 00:06:23,384
he brought four Navajo men to a

159
00:06:23,404 --> 00:06:25,368
demonstration in front of the base's

160
00:06:25,388 --> 00:06:26,710
commanding general, Clayton Vogel.

161
00:06:27,950 --> 00:06:30,272
Vogel's staff handed the Navajos six

162
00:06:30,292 --> 00:06:31,052
military messages.

163
00:06:31,072 --> 00:06:31,833
One read:

164
00:06:31,853 --> 00:06:34,565
Enemy expected to make tank and dive

165
00:06:34,585 --> 00:06:36,126
bomber attack at dawn.

166
00:06:36,146 --> 00:06:38,858
The Navajos passed it along in their

167
00:06:38,878 --> 00:06:41,980
language, and it came out the other end

168
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:42,371
as:

169
00:06:42,391 --> 00:06:45,103
Enemy tank dive bomber expected to attack

170
00:06:45,123 --> 00:06:45,883
this morning.

171
00:06:45,903 --> 00:06:46,664
Close enough.

172
00:06:46,684 --> 00:06:49,786
In a later test, a three-line message went

173
00:06:49,806 --> 00:06:51,347
through in twenty seconds.

174
00:06:51,367 --> 00:06:54,079
The coding machines of the day needed

175
00:06:54,099 --> 00:06:55,270
about thirty minutes.

176
00:06:56,550 --> 00:07:00,335
On the sixth of March, Vogel wrote to the

177
00:07:00,355 --> 00:07:02,449
Commandant of the Marine Corps.

178
00:07:02,469 --> 00:07:05,408
His letter survives in the National Archives.

179
00:07:05,428 --> 00:07:08,368
It passes on Johnston's claim that German

180
00:07:08,388 --> 00:07:10,904
students, posing as art students and

181
00:07:10,924 --> 00:07:13,018
anthropologists, had studied every tribal

182
00:07:13,038 --> 00:07:15,555
language in the country except Navajo.

183
00:07:15,575 --> 00:07:17,669
It says Navajo is completely

184
00:07:17,689 --> 00:07:20,628
unintelligible to all other tribes and all

185
00:07:20,648 --> 00:07:22,742
other people, except perhaps twenty-eight

186
00:07:22,762 --> 00:07:24,856
Americans who had studied it.

187
00:07:24,876 --> 00:07:25,702
And then:

188
00:07:25,722 --> 00:07:28,661
This dialect is thus equivalent to a

189
00:07:28,681 --> 00:07:30,775
secret code to the enemy.

190
00:07:30,795 --> 00:07:33,312
Vogel asked for two hundred Navajos.

191
00:07:33,332 --> 00:07:34,600
Headquarters approved thirty.

192
00:07:35,700 --> 00:07:38,800
On the morning of the fourth of May 1942,

193
00:07:38,820 --> 00:07:42,441
a group of young Navajo men climbed onto a

194
00:07:42,461 --> 00:07:44,464
bus at Fort Defiance, Arizona.

195
00:07:44,484 --> 00:07:46,487
At Fort Wingate, New Mexico,

196
00:07:46,507 --> 00:07:49,319
they were sworn into the United States

197
00:07:49,339 --> 00:07:50,128
Marine Corps.

198
00:07:50,148 --> 00:07:51,746
One had dropped out.

199
00:07:51,766 --> 00:07:52,980
Twenty-nine went on.

200
00:07:54,250 --> 00:07:56,335
The youngest, it is recorded,

201
00:07:56,355 --> 00:07:57,177
was fifteen.

202
00:07:57,197 --> 00:07:59,703
The oldest, Carl Gorman, was thirty-five.

203
00:07:59,723 --> 00:08:02,650
Chester Nez had kept his enlistment from

204
00:08:02,670 --> 00:08:03,492
his family.

205
00:08:03,512 --> 00:08:06,439
In San Diego they became Platoon three

206
00:08:06,459 --> 00:08:08,544
eighty-two, the first all-Navajo platoon

207
00:08:08,564 --> 00:08:11,090
in the history of the Corps.

208
00:08:12,375 --> 00:08:14,115
Nez's story was typical.

209
00:08:14,135 --> 00:08:18,332
He was born in 1921 at Chi Chil Tah,

210
00:08:18,352 --> 00:08:19,075
New Mexico.

211
00:08:19,095 --> 00:08:21,678
His mother died when he was three.

212
00:08:21,698 --> 00:08:23,909
At the government school, the teachers

213
00:08:23,929 --> 00:08:26,140
gave him a new name, Chester,

214
00:08:26,160 --> 00:08:27,628
after President Chester Arthur.

215
00:08:27,648 --> 00:08:30,231
He was recruited out of the boarding

216
00:08:30,251 --> 00:08:32,090
school at Tuba City, Arizona,

217
00:08:32,110 --> 00:08:35,065
the same kind of school where Navajo had

218
00:08:35,085 --> 00:08:35,808
been forbidden.

219
00:08:35,828 --> 00:08:38,411
Now the Marine Corps wanted him for

220
00:08:38,431 --> 00:08:39,231
exactly that.

221
00:08:40,400 --> 00:08:41,757
Look at the dates.

222
00:08:41,777 --> 00:08:44,166
The twenty-nine were sworn in on the

223
00:08:44,186 --> 00:08:45,199
fourth of May.

224
00:08:45,219 --> 00:08:47,960
On the sixth of May, Corregidor

225
00:08:47,980 --> 00:08:50,785
surrendered, and the American war in the

226
00:08:50,805 --> 00:08:51,996
Philippines was over.

227
00:08:52,016 --> 00:08:54,663
Joe Kieyoomia's prisoner of war card gives

228
00:08:54,683 --> 00:08:57,477
his date of capture as the seventh of May.

229
00:08:57,497 --> 00:08:59,978
In the same week, one group of Navajos

230
00:08:59,998 --> 00:09:01,854
began building a secret, and another

231
00:09:01,874 --> 00:09:04,042
Navajo became the prisoner who would be

232
00:09:04,062 --> 00:09:05,000
tortured for it.

233
00:09:07,550 --> 00:09:09,505
The platoon graduated at the end of June

234
00:09:09,525 --> 00:09:11,727
and was sent to Camp Elliott with one job:

235
00:09:11,747 --> 00:09:13,455
invent a code that the Japanese could

236
00:09:13,475 --> 00:09:15,429
never break, in a language that had never

237
00:09:15,449 --> 00:09:16,249
been written down.

238
00:09:17,875 --> 00:09:20,147
They started with what they knew.

239
00:09:20,167 --> 00:09:23,585
The first code had two hundred and eleven words.

240
00:09:23,605 --> 00:09:25,495
Instead of inventing new terms,

241
00:09:25,515 --> 00:09:28,169
they gave military things the names of

242
00:09:28,189 --> 00:09:29,335
things from home.

243
00:09:30,625 --> 00:09:32,665
A tank became chay-da-gahi,

244
00:09:32,685 --> 00:09:33,649
the tortoise.

245
00:09:33,669 --> 00:09:37,022
A submarine was besh-lo, the iron fish.

246
00:09:37,042 --> 00:09:40,419
A dive bomber was gini, the chicken hawk.

247
00:09:40,439 --> 00:09:43,105
A fighter plane was da-he-tih-hi,

248
00:09:43,125 --> 00:09:44,018
the hummingbird.

249
00:09:44,038 --> 00:09:46,302
A battleship was a whale.

250
00:09:46,322 --> 00:09:48,605
A destroyer was a shark.

251
00:09:49,900 --> 00:09:51,830
Countries got names too.

252
00:09:51,850 --> 00:09:54,754
Britain was the land between waters.

253
00:09:54,774 --> 00:09:57,679
The Philippines were the floating island.

254
00:09:57,699 --> 00:10:00,116
Germany was the iron hat.

255
00:10:00,136 --> 00:10:04,020
And America, in the code, was ne-he-mah:

256
00:10:04,040 --> 00:10:04,840
our mother.

257
00:10:05,675 --> 00:10:08,418
For words that had no code term,

258
00:10:08,438 --> 00:10:09,208
they spelled.

259
00:10:09,228 --> 00:10:11,971
Each English letter was a Navajo word.

260
00:10:11,991 --> 00:10:14,408
A could be wol-la-chee, the ant,

261
00:10:14,428 --> 00:10:16,715
or be-la-sana, the apple, or tse-nill,

262
00:10:16,735 --> 00:10:17,413
the axe.

263
00:10:17,433 --> 00:10:19,855
Using several words for each letter made

264
00:10:19,875 --> 00:10:22,297
the spelling harder to crack by counting

265
00:10:22,317 --> 00:10:24,391
how often a sound came up.

266
00:10:24,411 --> 00:10:26,135
The word Navy, for example,

267
00:10:26,155 --> 00:10:28,228
could go out as needle, ant,

268
00:10:28,248 --> 00:10:28,926
victor, yucca.

269
00:10:28,946 --> 00:10:31,368
So even a Navajo speaker listening in

270
00:10:31,388 --> 00:10:34,159
would hear words in his own language that

271
00:10:34,179 --> 00:10:35,575
meant nothing at all.

272
00:10:36,825 --> 00:10:39,783
That was the double lock on the code.

273
00:10:39,803 --> 00:10:43,134
To an outsider, Navajo was a wall of sound.

274
00:10:43,154 --> 00:10:46,112
To a Navajo who had not been trained,

275
00:10:46,132 --> 00:10:49,462
it was a string of birds, fish and animals

276
00:10:49,482 --> 00:10:50,579
with no meaning.

277
00:10:50,599 --> 00:10:51,324
Remember that.

278
00:10:51,344 --> 00:10:54,185
It is exactly what Joe Kieyoomia would face.

279
00:10:55,500 --> 00:10:58,444
The code grew as the war went on.

280
00:10:58,464 --> 00:11:01,038
It started with two hundred and eleven

281
00:11:01,058 --> 00:11:03,632
terms and reached more than four hundred.

282
00:11:03,652 --> 00:11:06,225
The code talkers learned it by heart.

283
00:11:06,245 --> 00:11:08,819
A Navy dictionary of the code survives

284
00:11:08,839 --> 00:11:12,480
from June 1945, and you can still read it today.

285
00:11:13,750 --> 00:11:15,594
The spelling alphabet grew too,

286
00:11:15,614 --> 00:11:17,458
from twenty-six words to forty-four.

287
00:11:17,478 --> 00:11:20,067
As the Marines moved across the Pacific,

288
00:11:20,087 --> 00:11:23,049
men from each division met in Hawaii to

289
00:11:23,069 --> 00:11:24,541
agree on new words.

290
00:11:24,561 --> 00:11:26,777
Speed mattered as much as secrecy.

291
00:11:26,797 --> 00:11:28,641
One of the original twenty-nine,

292
00:11:28,661 --> 00:11:30,505
Carl Gorman, put it simply.

293
00:11:30,525 --> 00:11:33,860
You had to be able to use English and

294
00:11:33,880 --> 00:11:34,978
Navajo pretty fast.

295
00:11:34,998 --> 00:11:37,215
Everybody was shooting at each other,

296
00:11:37,235 --> 00:11:40,590
and you really had to be on the ball.

297
00:11:41,900 --> 00:11:44,535
In one later test, run by a doubtful

298
00:11:44,555 --> 00:11:46,195
officer, the code talkers translated,

299
00:11:46,215 --> 00:11:48,850
sent and translated back a message in two

300
00:11:48,870 --> 00:11:50,177
and a half minutes.

301
00:11:50,197 --> 00:11:52,169
Without the code, he was told,

302
00:11:52,189 --> 00:11:54,180
the same job could take hours.

303
00:11:55,875 --> 00:11:59,636
In August 1942, the Marines landed on

304
00:11:59,656 --> 00:12:01,612
Guadalcanal in the Solomon Islands.

305
00:12:01,632 --> 00:12:04,379
The first Navajo code talkers came ashore

306
00:12:04,399 --> 00:12:06,375
near Lunga Point in September.

307
00:12:07,750 --> 00:12:09,795
Not every officer trusted them.

308
00:12:09,815 --> 00:12:12,273
The Marines' own director of recruiting

309
00:12:12,293 --> 00:12:15,164
had already objected to officers in combat

310
00:12:15,184 --> 00:12:17,641
depending on an order being translated.

311
00:12:17,661 --> 00:12:20,945
In the field, the code talkers had to

312
00:12:20,965 --> 00:12:23,030
prove themselves message by message.

313
00:12:24,350 --> 00:12:25,982
They had another problem.

314
00:12:26,002 --> 00:12:29,287
In the jungle, some Marines took them for

315
00:12:29,307 --> 00:12:29,700
Japanese.

316
00:12:29,720 --> 00:12:32,178
Code talker Bill Toledo later described

317
00:12:32,198 --> 00:12:34,657
being stopped by his own side.

318
00:12:34,677 --> 00:12:37,548
After that, a radio operator named Richard

319
00:12:37,568 --> 00:12:41,266
Bonham was assigned to stay with him as a

320
00:12:41,286 --> 00:12:41,679
bodyguard.

321
00:12:41,699 --> 00:12:43,745
Bonham later said that eventually,

322
00:12:43,765 --> 00:12:45,830
all the Navajos had bodyguards.

323
00:12:47,150 --> 00:12:49,952
There is a darker story often told about

324
00:12:49,972 --> 00:12:50,658
those bodyguards:

325
00:12:50,678 --> 00:12:53,480
that they had orders to shoot a code

326
00:12:53,500 --> 00:12:55,950
talker rather than let him be captured.

327
00:12:55,970 --> 00:12:58,067
Toledo said he was told so.

328
00:12:58,087 --> 00:13:01,242
A law passed by Congress in 2000 repeats it.

329
00:13:01,262 --> 00:13:04,064
But Bonham said no direct order was ever

330
00:13:04,084 --> 00:13:06,534
given, and the Marine Corps has always

331
00:13:06,554 --> 00:13:07,240
denied it.

332
00:13:07,260 --> 00:13:09,709
No written order has ever been found,

333
00:13:09,729 --> 00:13:12,884
and no code talker was ever shot by his

334
00:13:12,904 --> 00:13:13,704
own side.

335
00:13:14,900 --> 00:13:16,730
Meanwhile, Philip Johnston himself had

336
00:13:16,750 --> 00:13:19,690
joined the Marines as a staff sergeant at

337
00:13:19,710 --> 00:13:22,650
age fifty, to help train new code talkers

338
00:13:22,670 --> 00:13:23,760
at Camp Elliott.

339
00:13:23,780 --> 00:13:28,350
In June 1943, a magazine article by a

340
00:13:28,370 --> 00:13:30,721
government official revealed that Navajos

341
00:13:30,741 --> 00:13:33,091
were serving as Marine radiomen.

342
00:13:33,111 --> 00:13:35,461
Johnston was reprimanded and threatened

343
00:13:35,481 --> 00:13:36,884
with a court-martial.

344
00:13:36,904 --> 00:13:38,800
The secret held anyway.

345
00:13:40,175 --> 00:13:42,034
A Navajo communication school opened

346
00:13:42,054 --> 00:13:44,288
there, with formal classes from the

347
00:13:44,308 --> 00:13:48,379
seventh of December 1942, a year to the

348
00:13:48,399 --> 00:13:49,864
day after Pearl Harbor.

349
00:13:49,884 --> 00:13:52,090
The Marines asked for hundreds more

350
00:13:52,110 --> 00:13:54,317
Navajos, first at fifty a month,

351
00:13:54,337 --> 00:13:55,450
then at twenty-five.

352
00:13:56,550 --> 00:13:59,247
Back in the Philippines, Joe Kieyoomia was

353
00:13:59,267 --> 00:14:01,233
a prisoner at Camp O'Donnell.

354
00:14:01,253 --> 00:14:04,626
In 1995, he told an Associated Press

355
00:14:04,646 --> 00:14:07,572
reporter that he lived on rice laced with weevils.

356
00:14:07,592 --> 00:14:09,535
He said he watched weakened Americans

357
00:14:09,555 --> 00:14:11,826
being ordered to dig their own graves,

358
00:14:11,846 --> 00:14:13,810
and then shot by the guards.

359
00:14:15,175 --> 00:14:17,741
He said that at the end of one week,

360
00:14:17,761 --> 00:14:20,039
the guards told him he would be next.

361
00:14:20,059 --> 00:14:21,476
A few days later, instead,

362
00:14:21,496 --> 00:14:23,775
he was put on a ship to Japan.

363
00:14:23,795 --> 00:14:26,360
No record we have found names the ship or

364
00:14:26,380 --> 00:14:27,180
the date.

365
00:14:28,225 --> 00:14:28,965
Why him?

366
00:14:28,985 --> 00:14:31,860
Kieyoomia believed the Japanese had

367
00:14:31,880 --> 00:14:34,929
singled him out because of his face and

368
00:14:34,949 --> 00:14:35,697
his name.

369
00:14:35,717 --> 00:14:38,383
They thought he was a Japanese American,

370
00:14:38,403 --> 00:14:40,685
a traitor fighting for the enemy.

371
00:14:40,705 --> 00:14:43,371
He said they beat him for months.

372
00:14:43,391 --> 00:14:44,522
In his words:

373
00:14:44,542 --> 00:14:46,824
I told them I was Navajo.

374
00:14:46,844 --> 00:14:48,359
They didn't believe me.

375
00:14:48,379 --> 00:14:50,661
The only thing they understood about

376
00:14:50,681 --> 00:14:52,579
Americans was black and white.

377
00:14:52,599 --> 00:14:55,285
I guess they didn't know about Indians.

378
00:14:56,700 --> 00:14:59,390
In time, they accepted that he was Navajo.

379
00:14:59,410 --> 00:15:02,120
He said that was when things got worse.

380
00:15:03,850 --> 00:15:06,369
By then, Japanese radio monitors across

381
00:15:06,389 --> 00:15:08,908
the Pacific were hearing something they

382
00:15:08,928 --> 00:15:10,177
could not place.

383
00:15:10,197 --> 00:15:13,562
Marines were talking on open radio in a

384
00:15:13,582 --> 00:15:16,101
language no one in Japanese intelligence

385
00:15:16,121 --> 00:15:16,947
could identify.

386
00:15:16,967 --> 00:15:20,377
A 1981 history of the code talkers says

387
00:15:20,397 --> 00:15:23,139
the Japanese came to suspect a Native

388
00:15:23,159 --> 00:15:25,112
American language, and questioned American

389
00:15:25,132 --> 00:15:26,710
Indian prisoners about it.

390
00:15:27,925 --> 00:15:30,522
In Japan, they had a Navajo prisoner.

391
00:15:30,542 --> 00:15:32,765
In the accounts he gave later,

392
00:15:32,785 --> 00:15:35,556
Kieyoomia said he was brought before the

393
00:15:35,576 --> 00:15:38,089
camp commander and made to listen to

394
00:15:38,109 --> 00:15:39,899
recordings of American radio traffic.

395
00:15:39,919 --> 00:15:43,518
He said he could not believe what he was hearing.

396
00:15:43,538 --> 00:15:44,985
It sounded like Navajo.

397
00:15:46,325 --> 00:15:47,406
It was Navajo.

398
00:15:47,426 --> 00:15:49,240
But it was the code.

399
00:15:49,260 --> 00:15:52,174
He heard words he knew, one after another,

400
00:15:52,194 --> 00:15:54,742
in an order that made no sense.

401
00:15:54,762 --> 00:15:57,677
A history of the code talkers describes it

402
00:15:57,697 --> 00:16:00,265
as a completely random jumble of words.

403
00:16:01,550 --> 00:16:04,134
In the 1995 interview he described a

404
00:16:04,154 --> 00:16:04,886
different scene.

405
00:16:04,906 --> 00:16:07,519
Two Japanese women came to see him.

406
00:16:07,539 --> 00:16:10,151
They had written Navajo words in English

407
00:16:10,171 --> 00:16:13,160
letters, and they asked him what they meant.

408
00:16:13,180 --> 00:16:14,664
So he told them.

409
00:16:14,684 --> 00:16:16,920
This means bird, this means turtle,

410
00:16:16,940 --> 00:16:18,048
this means water.

411
00:16:18,068 --> 00:16:21,433
In the code, a bird could be a plane,

412
00:16:21,453 --> 00:16:22,937
a turtle a tank.

413
00:16:22,957 --> 00:16:25,570
Without the key, the words were exactly

414
00:16:25,590 --> 00:16:27,470
what he said they were.

415
00:16:28,675 --> 00:16:30,376
He told them the truth.

416
00:16:30,396 --> 00:16:32,441
I didn't know about the code,

417
00:16:32,461 --> 00:16:33,130
he said.

418
00:16:33,150 --> 00:16:35,215
They did not believe that either.

419
00:16:36,875 --> 00:16:38,587
What followed, in his accounts,

420
00:16:38,607 --> 00:16:39,280
was punishment.

421
00:16:39,300 --> 00:16:41,705
In one version, recorded by the author

422
00:16:41,725 --> 00:16:43,783
Sally McClain, an interrogator hit him

423
00:16:43,803 --> 00:16:46,208
with a club and broke his ribs,

424
00:16:46,228 --> 00:16:47,247
then his wrist.

425
00:16:47,267 --> 00:16:49,672
He said a British prisoner doctor named

426
00:16:49,692 --> 00:16:52,097
Whitfield examined him, but all he could

427
00:16:52,117 --> 00:16:55,235
do was bind his ribs and give him aspirin.

428
00:16:56,650 --> 00:16:59,904
Then came the winter day on the parade ground.

429
00:16:59,924 --> 00:17:01,359
It was twenty-seven degrees.

430
00:17:01,379 --> 00:17:03,542
One hour in the snow, naked,

431
00:17:03,562 --> 00:17:05,724
with a guard's rifle on him.

432
00:17:05,744 --> 00:17:07,543
When he was finally moved,

433
00:17:07,563 --> 00:17:10,453
the skin of his frozen feet tore away.

434
00:17:10,473 --> 00:17:13,364
His widow, years later, would put it in

435
00:17:13,384 --> 00:17:14,091
five words:

436
00:17:14,111 --> 00:17:15,930
he left his soles overseas.

437
00:17:17,325 --> 00:17:20,022
He said the beatings went on almost daily.

438
00:17:20,042 --> 00:17:23,417
For a time he could not use his right leg.

439
00:17:23,437 --> 00:17:25,794
He said he tried to starve himself,

440
00:17:25,814 --> 00:17:28,511
and the guards beat him until he ate.

441
00:17:28,531 --> 00:17:30,548
I wanted to die, he said.

442
00:17:30,568 --> 00:17:32,945
Many times, I thought I was close.

443
00:17:34,275 --> 00:17:36,897
There is one more detail in the accounts.

444
00:17:36,917 --> 00:17:39,539
The broadcasts he was forced to hear also

445
00:17:39,559 --> 00:17:40,530
gave him something.

446
00:17:40,550 --> 00:17:43,502
He could hear that his own people were out

447
00:17:43,522 --> 00:17:45,814
there on the radio, and that American

448
00:17:45,834 --> 00:17:47,815
forces were getting closer to Japan.

449
00:17:49,600 --> 00:17:51,500
We are about halfway through Joe

450
00:17:51,520 --> 00:17:52,520
Kieyoomia's story.

451
00:17:52,540 --> 00:17:55,560
If you want more prisoner of war stories

452
00:17:55,580 --> 00:17:58,620
told from the documents, subscribe to WWII

453
00:17:58,640 --> 00:17:59,440
Frontlines.

454
00:18:00,500 --> 00:18:04,401
In June 1944, the Marines landed on Saipan

455
00:18:04,421 --> 00:18:05,517
in the Marianas.

456
00:18:05,537 --> 00:18:07,750
A Marine Corps photograph shows Navajo

457
00:18:07,770 --> 00:18:09,238
communication men on Saipan,

458
00:18:09,258 --> 00:18:12,215
and its caption says they landed with the

459
00:18:12,235 --> 00:18:14,840
first assault waves to hit the beach.

460
00:18:16,175 --> 00:18:18,774
By then there were code talkers in every

461
00:18:18,794 --> 00:18:20,411
Marine division in the Pacific,

462
00:18:20,431 --> 00:18:23,030
and with the Raiders and the parachute units.

463
00:18:23,050 --> 00:18:25,649
A Navy history says they took part in

464
00:18:25,669 --> 00:18:27,940
every assault the Marines made in the

465
00:18:27,960 --> 00:18:32,315
Pacific from 1942 to 1945.

466
00:18:32,335 --> 00:18:33,624
At Bougainville, at Tarawa,

467
00:18:33,644 --> 00:18:36,243
at Guam, at Peleliu, the radio traffic of

468
00:18:36,263 --> 00:18:38,555
the Marine Corps was passing in Navajo.

469
00:18:39,925 --> 00:18:43,283
Being mistaken for the enemy did not stop.

470
00:18:43,303 --> 00:18:46,231
A 1981 history says that at least once,

471
00:18:46,251 --> 00:18:48,800
Marines under friendly fire had to put a

472
00:18:48,820 --> 00:18:51,048
code talker on the radio before the

473
00:18:51,068 --> 00:18:52,011
shelling was lifted.

474
00:18:52,031 --> 00:18:54,259
To the men on the other end,

475
00:18:54,279 --> 00:18:56,205
a Navajo voice was proof enough.

476
00:18:57,875 --> 00:19:00,635
On the nineteenth of February 1945,

477
00:19:00,655 --> 00:19:02,945
the Marines landed on Iwo Jima,

478
00:19:02,965 --> 00:19:05,255
an island of black volcanic ash.

479
00:19:05,275 --> 00:19:07,950
Major Howard Connor was the signal officer

480
00:19:07,970 --> 00:19:09,895
of the Fifth Marine Division.

481
00:19:11,275 --> 00:19:13,388
According to Connor, six Navajo code

482
00:19:13,408 --> 00:19:15,876
talkers worked around the clock during the

483
00:19:15,896 --> 00:19:18,008
first two days of the battle.

484
00:19:18,028 --> 00:19:20,496
They sent and received more than eight

485
00:19:20,516 --> 00:19:21,207
hundred messages.

486
00:19:21,227 --> 00:19:23,715
Not one of them had an error.

487
00:19:25,050 --> 00:19:27,623
Connor's verdict has been quoted ever since.

488
00:19:27,643 --> 00:19:29,846
Were it not for the Navajos,

489
00:19:29,866 --> 00:19:32,810
the Marines would never have taken Iwo Jima.

490
00:19:32,830 --> 00:19:35,403
A captain's report from the battle called

491
00:19:35,423 --> 00:19:37,256
the Navajo code the simplest,

492
00:19:37,276 --> 00:19:39,849
fastest and most reliable means of passing

493
00:19:39,869 --> 00:19:40,669
a message.

494
00:19:41,950 --> 00:19:45,421
By the end of the war, about four hundred

495
00:19:45,441 --> 00:19:47,748
Navajos had served as code talkers.

496
00:19:47,768 --> 00:19:49,687
Thirteen were killed in action.

497
00:19:49,707 --> 00:19:52,014
One of them was Harry Tsosie,

498
00:19:52,034 --> 00:19:54,729
from Rough Rock, Arizona, one of the

499
00:19:54,749 --> 00:19:55,549
original twenty-nine.

500
00:19:56,225 --> 00:19:59,284
They were not the only Native Americans in

501
00:19:59,304 --> 00:19:59,669
uniform.

502
00:19:59,689 --> 00:20:01,594
About forty-four thousand Native Americans

503
00:20:01,614 --> 00:20:03,904
served in the Second World War,

504
00:20:03,924 --> 00:20:05,828
by the White House count.

505
00:20:05,848 --> 00:20:08,523
One history puts the number of Navajos

506
00:20:08,543 --> 00:20:10,447
alone at about thirty-six hundred.

507
00:20:10,467 --> 00:20:12,757
Choctaw, Chippewa, Creek, Sioux and other

508
00:20:12,777 --> 00:20:15,451
men also passed messages in their languages.

509
00:20:15,471 --> 00:20:18,916
But the Navajo code was the one built for

510
00:20:18,936 --> 00:20:21,610
the whole Marine Corps, in every division

511
00:20:21,630 --> 00:20:22,785
in the Pacific.

512
00:20:24,175 --> 00:20:26,811
And Japan never broke the code.

513
00:20:26,831 --> 00:20:29,910
After the war, Japan's chief of army

514
00:20:29,930 --> 00:20:32,255
intelligence, General Seizo Arisue,

515
00:20:32,275 --> 00:20:35,379
said that his men had been able to read

516
00:20:35,399 --> 00:20:37,115
codes used by the U.S.

517
00:20:37,135 --> 00:20:39,198
Army and the Army Air Forces.

518
00:20:39,218 --> 00:20:40,934
The Marines' code, he said,

519
00:20:40,954 --> 00:20:41,995
they never cracked.

520
00:20:43,700 --> 00:20:47,100
The ninth of August 1945.

521
00:20:47,120 --> 00:20:49,602
An American bomber dropped the second

522
00:20:49,622 --> 00:20:51,269
atomic bomb on Nagasaki.

523
00:20:51,289 --> 00:20:53,750
Where was Joe Kieyoomia?

524
00:20:54,450 --> 00:20:56,203
Here the accounts split.

525
00:20:56,223 --> 00:20:59,934
In his 1995 interview, Kieyoomia said he

526
00:20:59,954 --> 00:21:02,831
was in a prison cell in Nagasaki when the

527
00:21:02,851 --> 00:21:03,475
bomb fell.

528
00:21:03,495 --> 00:21:06,050
He said the concrete walls of the cell

529
00:21:06,070 --> 00:21:06,694
protected him.

530
00:21:06,714 --> 00:21:09,591
He said he was left alone there for three

531
00:21:09,611 --> 00:21:11,844
days, until a Japanese officer came and

532
00:21:11,864 --> 00:21:12,830
set him free.

533
00:21:14,125 --> 00:21:15,648
But in an earlier interview,

534
00:21:15,668 --> 00:21:17,945
published in 1994 by Sally McClain,

535
00:21:17,965 --> 00:21:19,278
he said something different.

536
00:21:19,298 --> 00:21:22,612
He said he had been moved to a camp he

537
00:21:22,632 --> 00:21:25,389
called Matishima, about sixty miles south

538
00:21:25,409 --> 00:21:27,585
of Nagasaki, the day before the bomb.

539
00:21:28,900 --> 00:21:31,520
The American records add a third version.

540
00:21:31,540 --> 00:21:34,825
Kieyoomia's prisoner of war card lists

541
00:21:34,845 --> 00:21:38,486
him, at the end, at a camp in the Tokyo area.

542
00:21:38,506 --> 00:21:41,483
That may only be where his paperwork was closed.

543
00:21:41,503 --> 00:21:43,500
It does not settle the question.

544
00:21:44,850 --> 00:21:47,170
So we cannot tell you for certain that Joe

545
00:21:47,190 --> 00:21:49,417
Kieyoomia lived through the atomic bomb.

546
00:21:49,437 --> 00:21:52,633
What we can tell you is that he lived

547
00:21:52,653 --> 00:21:53,705
through the war.

548
00:21:53,725 --> 00:21:56,206
His card says he returned to American

549
00:21:56,226 --> 00:22:00,510
control on the thirteenth of September 1945.

550
00:22:00,530 --> 00:22:03,144
By the calendar, he had been a prisoner

551
00:22:03,164 --> 00:22:04,461
for about forty-one months.

552
00:22:04,481 --> 00:22:06,436
He and his family always counted

553
00:22:06,456 --> 00:22:09,090
forty-three, perhaps to the day he got home.

554
00:22:10,750 --> 00:22:12,638
All across Japan that September,

555
00:22:12,658 --> 00:22:15,692
American planes and ships came for the prisoners.

556
00:22:15,712 --> 00:22:17,982
In Yokohama, Army cameramen filmed them:

557
00:22:18,002 --> 00:22:20,272
thin, grinning men in new uniforms,

558
00:22:20,292 --> 00:22:22,562
eating, smoking, climbing into the planes

559
00:22:22,582 --> 00:22:24,490
that would take them home.

560
00:22:25,775 --> 00:22:28,023
According to the book written with code

561
00:22:28,043 --> 00:22:30,052
talker Samuel Holiday, Kieyoomia spent

562
00:22:30,072 --> 00:22:32,432
about four years in different veterans'

563
00:22:32,452 --> 00:22:36,003
hospitals before he was well enough to go home.

564
00:22:36,023 --> 00:22:37,194
His leg recovered.

565
00:22:37,214 --> 00:22:38,384
His feet healed.

566
00:22:38,404 --> 00:22:40,765
He went back to the reservation,

567
00:22:40,785 --> 00:22:41,975
and to silver.

568
00:22:43,350 --> 00:22:45,725
The code talkers came home too,

569
00:22:45,745 --> 00:22:46,125
quietly.

570
00:22:46,145 --> 00:22:48,520
A National Archives history says they

571
00:22:48,540 --> 00:22:50,516
returned on buses, without parades.

572
00:22:50,536 --> 00:22:52,512
Their work was still classified.

573
00:22:52,532 --> 00:22:55,706
Bill Toledo said they just told him to

574
00:22:55,726 --> 00:22:58,900
keep his mouth shut about what he did.

575
00:22:58,920 --> 00:23:00,098
So they did.

576
00:23:00,118 --> 00:23:03,292
For years, many of their own families did

577
00:23:03,312 --> 00:23:04,112
not know.

578
00:23:05,375 --> 00:23:08,006
Of the New Mexico gunners who went to the

579
00:23:08,026 --> 00:23:11,068
Philippines in 1941, about nine hundred

580
00:23:11,088 --> 00:23:12,042
came back alive.

581
00:23:12,062 --> 00:23:14,962
A state history says a third of those died

582
00:23:14,982 --> 00:23:15,955
within a year.

583
00:23:17,650 --> 00:23:20,262
The Navajo code stayed classified for

584
00:23:20,282 --> 00:23:22,456
twenty-three years after the war.

585
00:23:22,476 --> 00:23:26,730
It was finally declassified in 1968.

586
00:23:26,750 --> 00:23:30,175
In 1971, President Nixon sent the code

587
00:23:30,195 --> 00:23:32,021
talkers a letter of thanks.

588
00:23:32,041 --> 00:23:35,430
In 1982, President Reagan declared the

589
00:23:35,450 --> 00:23:37,560
fourteenth of August National Navajo Code

590
00:23:37,580 --> 00:23:38,380
Talkers Day.

591
00:23:39,600 --> 00:23:41,871
Reagan's proclamation went further than

592
00:23:41,891 --> 00:23:43,245
any general had.

593
00:23:43,265 --> 00:23:45,995
It called theirs the only foolproof,

594
00:23:46,015 --> 00:23:49,202
unbreakable code in the history of warfare.

595
00:23:49,222 --> 00:23:53,702
In 1976, the code talkers had marched in

596
00:23:53,722 --> 00:23:55,396
the nation's bicentennial parade.

597
00:23:55,416 --> 00:23:58,707
In 1981, the Navajo chairman and former

598
00:23:58,727 --> 00:24:00,564
Marine Peter MacDonald said of them:

599
00:24:00,584 --> 00:24:02,440
we can never thank them enough.

600
00:24:03,700 --> 00:24:06,220
Kieyoomia said he only learned about the

601
00:24:06,240 --> 00:24:08,260
code talkers years after the war.

602
00:24:08,280 --> 00:24:11,643
And there is a story, printed in 1981 and

603
00:24:11,663 --> 00:24:13,980
again by the Navy in 1992,

604
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:16,947
about a Navajo soldier who had been a

605
00:24:16,967 --> 00:24:18,060
prisoner from Bataan.

606
00:24:18,080 --> 00:24:21,769
After the war, he met one of the code talkers.

607
00:24:21,789 --> 00:24:23,995
The sources don't give his name.

608
00:24:24,015 --> 00:24:26,680
But what he said fits Joe Kieyoomia

609
00:24:26,700 --> 00:24:26,954
exactly:

610
00:24:26,974 --> 00:24:29,421
I never figured out what you guys who got

611
00:24:29,441 --> 00:24:31,360
me into all that trouble were saying.

612
00:24:32,675 --> 00:24:34,639
He never blamed them.

613
00:24:34,659 --> 00:24:38,428
In 1995, sitting outside his home on the

614
00:24:38,448 --> 00:24:40,088
reservation in northwestern New Mexico,

615
00:24:40,108 --> 00:24:41,749
with his twelve service medals,

616
00:24:41,769 --> 00:24:43,077
he told the reporter:

617
00:24:43,097 --> 00:24:44,738
I salute the code talkers.

618
00:24:44,758 --> 00:24:47,394
And even if I knew about their code,

619
00:24:47,414 --> 00:24:49,075
I wouldn't tell the Japanese.

620
00:24:50,900 --> 00:24:52,920
For the rest of his life, Joe Kieyoomia

621
00:24:52,940 --> 00:24:54,585
was a silversmith.

622
00:24:54,605 --> 00:24:57,753
In 1983, a National Geographic writer who

623
00:24:57,773 --> 00:25:00,460
came through the Shiprock area wrote that

624
00:25:00,480 --> 00:25:03,106
Kieyoomia agreed to show him his craft.

625
00:25:03,126 --> 00:25:06,415
He was listed for years in a directory of

626
00:25:06,435 --> 00:25:07,518
Native American artists:

627
00:25:07,538 --> 00:25:08,253
Joe L.

628
00:25:08,273 --> 00:25:09,844
Kieyoomia, Navajo, jewelry,

629
00:25:09,864 --> 00:25:11,220
Shiprock, New Mexico.

630
00:25:12,550 --> 00:25:15,446
He was married to his wife Lita for

631
00:25:15,466 --> 00:25:16,175
forty-two years.

632
00:25:16,195 --> 00:25:19,091
They had eight children, and by the end

633
00:25:19,111 --> 00:25:20,549
there were fourteen grandchildren.

634
00:25:20,569 --> 00:25:23,100
He belonged to the veterans' post in

635
00:25:23,120 --> 00:25:25,652
Aztec, and to the association of the

636
00:25:25,672 --> 00:25:28,450
survivors of Bataan and Corregidor.

637
00:25:29,150 --> 00:25:31,490
Joe Kieyoomia died on the seventeenth of

638
00:25:31,510 --> 00:25:34,404
February 1997, at the hospital in Shiprock.

639
00:25:34,424 --> 00:25:37,055
The Farmington paper gave his age as

640
00:25:37,075 --> 00:25:39,707
eighty-four, which does not match his Army

641
00:25:39,727 --> 00:25:40,465
card either.

642
00:25:40,485 --> 00:25:42,358
He was buried in Farmington,

643
00:25:42,378 --> 00:25:45,030
with a military service at the grave.

644
00:25:46,375 --> 00:25:49,822
In 2002, at the dedication of the Bataan

645
00:25:49,842 --> 00:25:51,935
Memorial in Albuquerque, Lita Kieyoomia

646
00:25:51,955 --> 00:25:53,571
spoke about her husband.

647
00:25:53,591 --> 00:25:55,617
He left his soles overseas,

648
00:25:55,637 --> 00:25:56,455
she said.

649
00:25:58,100 --> 00:26:01,219
In December 2000, Congress passed a law to

650
00:26:01,239 --> 00:26:02,789
honor the code talkers.

651
00:26:02,809 --> 00:26:05,144
The original twenty-nine would receive the

652
00:26:05,164 --> 00:26:06,321
Congressional Gold Medal.

653
00:26:06,341 --> 00:26:09,461
Every man who had qualified as a code

654
00:26:09,481 --> 00:26:12,620
talker after them would receive a silver one.

655
00:26:13,900 --> 00:26:16,187
On the twenty-sixth of July 2001,

656
00:26:16,207 --> 00:26:18,493
in the Rotunda of the Capitol,

657
00:26:18,513 --> 00:26:19,647
President George W.

658
00:26:19,667 --> 00:26:21,569
Bush presented the gold medals.

659
00:26:21,589 --> 00:26:24,644
By then, only five of the twenty-nine were

660
00:26:24,664 --> 00:26:28,104
alive, and four of them were able to come:

661
00:26:28,124 --> 00:26:30,796
John Brown, Chester Nez, Lloyd Oliver and

662
00:26:30,816 --> 00:26:31,949
Allen Dale June.

663
00:26:31,969 --> 00:26:34,660
Joe Palmer was represented by his son.

664
00:26:35,950 --> 00:26:38,430
The President told the room that the code

665
00:26:38,450 --> 00:26:40,617
word for America had been our mother.

666
00:26:40,637 --> 00:26:43,116
He said they had given their country a

667
00:26:43,136 --> 00:26:44,678
service only they could give.

668
00:26:44,698 --> 00:26:47,490
And he said that at home, they had carried

669
00:26:47,510 --> 00:26:50,010
for decades the secret of their own heroism.

670
00:26:51,350 --> 00:26:54,490
When John Brown spoke, he used a few words

671
00:26:54,510 --> 00:26:55,192
of Navajo.

672
00:26:55,212 --> 00:26:56,246
Then he added:

673
00:26:56,266 --> 00:26:57,670
Maybe Japan is listening.

674
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:01,450
Chester Nez, the last of the original

675
00:27:01,470 --> 00:27:04,273
twenty-nine, died in 2014 at the age of

676
00:27:04,293 --> 00:27:04,626
ninety-three.

677
00:27:04,646 --> 00:27:07,802
In an interview in 2002, he had summed up

678
00:27:07,822 --> 00:27:09,567
the war in one sentence.

679
00:27:09,587 --> 00:27:12,037
The Japanese pulled out all their hair,

680
00:27:12,057 --> 00:27:14,880
he said, but they never could break it.

681
00:27:16,075 --> 00:27:18,762
As of August 2026, the Associated Press

682
00:27:18,782 --> 00:27:21,469
counts only two Navajo code talkers still

683
00:27:21,489 --> 00:27:21,856
alive:

684
00:27:21,876 --> 00:27:24,950
Thomas Begay, who is one hundred and one,

685
00:27:24,970 --> 00:27:27,270
and Peter MacDonald, who joined the

686
00:27:27,290 --> 00:27:28,450
Marines at fifteen.

687
00:27:29,150 --> 00:27:32,416
Their story is still being argued over and

688
00:27:32,436 --> 00:27:32,827
remembered.

689
00:27:32,847 --> 00:27:36,113
In 2025, pages about the code talkers were

690
00:27:36,133 --> 00:27:38,166
removed from Defense Department websites,

691
00:27:38,186 --> 00:27:41,041
and then put back, with the Pentagon

692
00:27:41,061 --> 00:27:43,506
saying they had been erroneously removed.

693
00:27:43,526 --> 00:27:46,792
In August 2026, plans were announced for a

694
00:27:46,812 --> 00:27:48,846
code talkers museum in Farmington,

695
00:27:48,866 --> 00:27:51,910
New Mexico, the town where Joe Kieyoomia

696
00:27:51,930 --> 00:27:52,730
is buried.

697
00:27:54,075 --> 00:27:56,784
If someone in your family served with the

698
00:27:56,804 --> 00:27:59,512
code talkers, or was a prisoner of the

699
00:27:59,532 --> 00:28:02,241
Japanese, tell us their story in the comments.

700
00:28:02,261 --> 00:28:03,625
We read every one.

701
00:28:04,725 --> 00:28:07,098
On the high desert of northwestern New

702
00:28:07,118 --> 00:28:10,517
Mexico, a great dark rock rises out of the plain.

703
00:28:10,537 --> 00:28:12,568
In English it is called Shiprock.

704
00:28:12,588 --> 00:28:15,645
It gave its name to the town where Joe

705
00:28:15,665 --> 00:28:16,802
Kieyoomia made his silver,

706
00:28:16,822 --> 00:28:17,885
and where he died.

707
00:28:19,225 --> 00:28:21,549
The language spoken on that land was once

708
00:28:21,569 --> 00:28:22,720
punished in its schools.

709
00:28:22,740 --> 00:28:25,357
In the war, it became the one code the

710
00:28:25,377 --> 00:28:26,236
enemy never broke.

711
00:28:26,256 --> 00:28:28,872
And of all the people who heard it during

712
00:28:28,892 --> 00:28:31,216
the war, the one who could have helped

713
00:28:31,236 --> 00:28:33,266
Japan the most was a Navajo prisoner

714
00:28:33,286 --> 00:28:35,610
standing in the snow, who knew every word,

715
00:28:35,630 --> 00:28:37,074
and told them the truth:

716
00:28:37,094 --> 00:28:39,145
that he did not understand it either.

